let's think about where we would plot  two sixths on this number line so let's  count what each of these little marks  represent so we have when we're going  between zero and one we have one  two three four five  six of these marks or another way to  think of it is these marks split zero  the the length of our number line  between zero and one into exactly six  equal spaces so this is one of the six  then this is another of the six so that  gets us to 2 6. if we go another one we  get to 3 6. we go another one we get to  4 6 then 5 6 then this is 6 6 which is  the same thing as 1. so to answer our  question where is 2 6 well this gets us  to 1 6 then another 6 gets us 2 2 6. if  someone were to ask you where is 5 6  well that is 1 6 2 6 3 6 4 6 and 5 6. if  someone were to ask you where is three  over six well once again this these and  you have to make sure this is splitting  up the number line to exactly six equal  spaces between zero and one but if you  do then these are each sixth and so you  get 1 6 2 6 and 3 6 and you may or may  not know 3 6 is the same thing as one  half if you divide 3 by 3 and 6 by 3  you're dividing by dividing the  numerator and denominator by the same  thing you get one-half and that makes  sense because this is exactly one-half  of the way between zero and one
 

